The plural possessive form of "dog" is "dogs'". For example, "The dogs' toys are always lost." This is because when you add an apostrophe and an 's' to the end of a word, it indicates possession. So in this case, it shows that the toys belong to the dogs. 
However, it is important to note that in some cases, such as when referring to multiple people, the possessive form is not used with an apostrophe. For example, if you were talking about multiple cats, you would say "The cats' toys" instead of "The cats' toys". This is because in this case, it is not clear who owns what without an apostrophe. 
In summary, the plural possessive form of "dog" is "dogs'" and not "dog's". It is important to use caution when referring to multiple people in order to avoid confusion. 
